What I would give the coding agent

I'd tell it to approach this as four separate systems, not one giant change:

Phase 1 — Roadside generation

Fix road-relative placement and create the frontage → small buildings → large buildings hierarchy.

Phase 2 — New streaming

Replace the current building loading system with road-chunk streaming.

Phase 3 — Vehicle system

Add the F1 asset as a separate vehicle option and implement a dedicated F1 controller.

Phase 4 — Developer tuning

Create a common live-tuning layer for both vehicles, with Numpad controls and an on-screen debug panel.

And don't touch the existing driving system more than necessary during phases 1–2. You said it's "good enough," so I'd avoid turning a working system into a physics rewrite while you're fixing the world-generation problems.

The biggest architectural change I'd insist on is:

The road owns the city generation. The city does not independently choose where it wants to exist.

That one decision should eliminate most of the "buildings floating on the road / buildings too far away" behavior you're seeing.